## 1. Introduction

This guide outlines the process for creating detail components using the uu5Generate command.

### 1.1 When to Use

Use this guide when creating components for:
- Entity detail views (e.g., Library Detail, Note Detail)
- Single object data display with proper layout
- Hybrid InfoGroup and Grid implementations

### 1.2 Fundamental Rule

⚠️ **ABSOLUTE MANDATORY REQUIREMENT** ⚠️

**NO IMPLEMENTATION BEFORE USER CONFIRMATION - ZERO EXCEPTIONS!**

Before writing ANY code, you MUST ALWAYS follow these steps IN ORDER:

1. 🛑 **FIRST STEP - CONFIRM INTENT**: 
   - ASK the user to CONFIRM they want to create a detail component
   - Verify the module name (e.g., `Note`, `Library`)
   - Verify the component name (e.g., `Detail`, `VersionTagDetail`)
   - WAIT for explicit YES/NO confirmation

2. 🛑 **SECOND STEP - GATHER SPECIFICATIONS** (only after step 1 is confirmed):
   - ASK for object/entity description or uuSchema documentation link
   - ASK about baseUri configuration
   - ASK about load/get command details
   - ASK about identifier attribute
   - ASK about any special requirements

3. 🛑 **THIRD STEP - FINAL CONFIRMATION**:
   - SUMMARIZE all gathered specifications
   - ASK for final confirmation before implementation
   - WAIT for explicit approval

4. 🛑 DO NOT proceed with ANY implementation until ALL confirmations are received.

> **DETAIL COMPONENT RULE**  
> When a user wants to create a new **detail component**, do NOT generate any files manually.  
> Instead, use the uu5Generate command in the **-core library**:
> ```bash
> npm run uu5Generate detailComponent <module>.<name> -- --sub-app=<value> --brick-uu5-tag=<value> --redirect-uu5-route=<value>
> ```


**⚠️ WARNING**: NEVER call uu5Generate without ALL parameters - it will prompt for interactive input!

**IMPORTANT**: ALWAYS use cmd when calling uu5Generate. Do NOT use PowerShell as it causes syntax issues with the command execution.

