## 3. Generation Process

### 3.1 Command Parameters

**Optional Parameters** (with default values):
> • **sub-app** [string] – the sub app of the corresponding uuApp  
>   *Default: derived from library name (e.g. `uu_frontend_aicodingg01-core` → `uu_frontend_aicodingg01`)*  
>   *This option is **mandatory** for all cases*
> • **brick-uu5-tag** [string] – full uu5Tag of related brick component for "Copy component" functionality  
>   *Default: `"-"` (taken as empty when used)*  
>   *This option is **mandatory** when generate-brick is positive*
> • **generate-brick** [bool] – whether to generate related brick component  
>   *This is a **mandatory** option with no default value*  
>   *Use `no-generate-brick` when user doesn't want to generate brick component*
> • **redirect-uu5-route** [string] – related uu5Route for "Redirect / open in new window" functionality  
>   *Default: `"-"`*
> 
> **Process**:
> 1. **ALWAYS provide ALL parameters** when calling uu5Generate - never call it without parameters
> 2. **Use default values** for all parameters unless user specifies otherwise
> 3. **Execute the uu5Generate command** with ALL parameters specified
> 
> **CRITICAL**: NEVER call `npm run uu5Generate detailComponent <module>.<name>` without parameters. ALWAYS include all required parameters: `--sub-app`, `--brick-uu5-tag`, and `--redirect-uu5-route`.
> 
> **Examples**:
> ```bash
> # CORRECT: Using default values (all parameters MUST be specified)
> # ALWAYS use cmd, NOT PowerShell
> cmd /c "npm run uu5Generate detailComponent Note.Detail -- --sub-app=uu_frontend_aicodingg01 --brick-uu5-tag=- --redirect-uu5-route=-"
> 
> # CORRECT: Overriding specific parameters
> cmd /c "npm run uu5Generate detailComponent Note.Detail -- --sub-app=mySubApp --brick-uu5-tag=UuMyAppBricks.Note.Detail --redirect-uu5-route=noteDetail"
> 
> # CORRECT: When not generating brick component
> cmd /c "npm run uu5Generate detailComponent Note.Detail -- --sub-app=uu_frontend_aicodingg01 --no-generate-brick --redirect-uu5-route=-"
> 
> # WRONG: Never call without parameters - this will prompt for input
> # npm run uu5Generate detailComponent Note.Detail
> ```
> 
> The uu5Generate command will automatically create all necessary files including:
> - Component files (js, test, html demo)
> - Proper ContentContainer integration
> - Registration in uuapp.json
- Brick component generation (if applicable)

### 3.2 Object Description
> After the detail component is successfully generated, you MUST check if the user already provided object documentation in their initial request. If they did, use that information directly. If not, ask the user for the object that will be represented by the detail component.
> 
> **Check first**: Look for uuSchema documentation links or object descriptions in the user's initial prompt
> 
> **If not provided, ask the user to provide**:
> • **Object Description** – Either a text description of the object/entity that the detail component will represent, OR
> • **Documentation Link** – A link to the uuSchema documentation that describes the object structure
> 
> **Purpose**: This information will be used to:
> - Implement the content component with proper data fields
> - Configure the component's data mapping
> - Set up appropriate validation and business logic
> - Create meaningful demo data
> 
> **Example questions to ask**:
> - "What object/entity will this detail component represent? Please provide either a text description or a link to the uuSchema documentation."
> - "What data fields and properties should this detail component display for the [Module] entity?"
- "Do you have a uuSchema documentation link that describes the structure of the [Module] object?"

### 3.3 Load/Get Command Verification
> After receiving the object description, you MUST check the provided documentation for the load command to verify the correct identifier attribute.
> 
> **Primary verification - Load command**:
> - Look for the `[module]/load` command in the documentation (e.g., `library/load`)
> - Verify what identifier attribute is used in `dtoIn` (e.g., `id` or `oid`)
> - Check the command signature to confirm the expected input parameters
> 
> **Fallback verification - Get command**:
> - If `[module]/load` command is not found, look for `[module]/get` command (e.g., `library/get`)
> - Verify what identifier attribute is used in `dtoIn` for the get command
> - Check the command signature to confirm the expected input parameters
> - **Note**: Get commands typically use different identifier attributes than load commands
> 
> **STEP AFTER LOAD/GET COMMAND VERIFICATION - Object ID Configuration**:
> Based on the load or get command documentation, configure the correct identifier attribute. If the command uses `id` instead of `oid`, you MUST:
> 
> 1. **Change `oid` prop to `id`** in the main detail component (`src/[module]/detail.js`)
> 2. **Change `oid` prop to `id`** in the provider component (`src/[module]/provider.js`)
> 3. **Change `oid` prop to `id`** in the use-provider hook (`src/[module]/use-provider.js`)
> 4. **Update propTypes** to use `id` instead of `oid` in all affected components
> 5. **Update the provider propTypes** to reflect the correct identifier field
> 6. **Update the demo page** (`demo/[module]/detail.html`) to use the correct identifier:
>    - Change the `oid` prop to the correct identifier (e.g., `id`, `code`)
>    - Update any hardcoded demo values to use the correct identifier format
   - Ensure the demo page works with the new identifier structure

### 3.4 Object ID Configuration

Based on the load or get command documentation, configure the correct identifier attribute as described above.

## 4. Content Implementation

### 4.1 BaseUri Configuration
> After configuring the object ID, you MUST ask the user about baseUri configuration:
> 
> **Ask the user**:
> - "Will the baseUri be the same for workspace check and for the [Module] object loading?"
> - "If different, what baseUri should be used for [Module] object loading?"
> - "Do you want to remove the WorkspaceProvider and use only baseUri for [Module] object loading? (workspace state check is not needed)"
> 
> **Purpose**: This determines whether to use the same baseUri for both workspace validation and object data loading, or if separate baseUris are needed for different operations.
> 
> **Implementation options**:
> - **Option 1 - Same baseUri**: Use the existing `baseUri` prop for both workspace and object operations
> - **Option 2 - Different baseUri**: Define TWO separate props:
>   - `baseUri` - for workspace check operations
>   - `objectBaseUri` - for object loading operations
>   - **Update the detail component** to pass the correct baseUri to each provider:
>     - `baseUri` prop → passed to `WorkspaceProvider` as `baseUri`
>     - `objectBaseUri` prop → passed to `[Module]Provider` as `baseUri`
> - **Option 3 - Remove WorkspaceProvider**: Remove WorkspaceProvider entirely and use only baseUri for [Module] object loading:
>   - Remove `WorkspaceProvider` import and usage from detail component
>   - Pass `baseUri` directly to `[Module]Provider`
>   - Update propTypes to use `[Module]Provider.propTypes.baseUri` instead of `WorkspaceProvider.propTypes.baseUri`
>   - **Clean up unused workspace components**: If WorkspaceProvider is not used elsewhere, delete the generated workspace-related files:
>     - `src/workspace/workspace.js`
>     - `src/workspace/config/config.js`
>     - `src/workspace/provider.js`
>     - `src/workspace/use-workspace.js`
>     - Remove workspace-related calls from `src/calls.js`
    - Remove workspace exports from `src/exports.js` and `src/index.js`

### 4.2 Hybrid Content Implementation
> After completing the baseUri configuration, you MUST implement the content component using a hybrid approach with InfoGroup for basic fields and Grid layout for nested objects.
> 
> **Implementation Structure**:
> - **InfoGroup**: Display basic fields (OID, name, description, etc.) in a single InfoGroup
> - **Grid Layout**: Display nested objects (maps, arrays) in responsive grid layouts below the InfoGroup
> - **Spacing**: Add proper spacing between InfoGroup and grid sections
> 
> **Key steps**:
> 1. Open `src/[module]/detail/content.js`
> 2. Import InfoGroup, Grid, and Text from `uu5g05-elements`
> 3. Replace PlaceholderBox with hybrid structure (InfoGroup + Grid)
> 4. **Basic Fields (InfoGroup)**:
>    - Use `{ subtitle: "Label", title: "Value" }` for basic fields
>    - Use `href: url` directly in the item object for clickable links
>    - **Always make URI fields clickable** by detecting URIs and adding `href` property
>    - **Create helper function** to automatically detect URIs and conditionally add `href`
>    - **Exclude system attributes**: Do NOT display sysCts, sysMts, sysRev, or other system-generated fields
> 5. **Nested Objects (Grid Layout)**:
>    - Use ListLayout for each nested object (maps, arrays)
>    - Structure: `{ label: "Section Name", children: <ListLayout itemList={[...]} /> }`
>    - For arrays, map each item with proper labeling
>    - Use nested ListLayout for complex nested structures
> 6. **Spacing**: Add `marginTop: "24px"` between InfoGroup and grid sections
> 7. Handle data formatting and display
> 
> **Data Access Pattern**:
> Always check both `[module]Dto` and `[module]Dto.data` when accessing data:
> ```javascript
> // Check both [module]Dto and [module]Dto.data for data
> const data = [module]Dto.data || [module]Dto;
> 
> if (!data) {
>   return null;
> }
> ```

> **Example Implementation**:
> ```javascript
> // Basic fields in InfoGroup
> const itemList = [
>   { subtitle: lsi.oid, ...addHrefIfUri(data.oid) },
>   { subtitle: lsi.name, ...addHrefIfUri(data.name) },
>   // ... more basic fields
> ];
> 
> // Nested objects in ListLayout
> const listLayoutItems = [
>   {
>     label: lsi.nestedObjectName,
>     children: (
>       <ListLayout
>         itemList={[
>           { label: lsi.field1, children: nestedObject.field1 },
>           { label: lsi.field2, children: nestedObject.field2 },
>         ]}
>       />
>     ),
>   },
> ];
> 
> return (
>   <>
>     <InfoGroup header={lsi.details} itemList={itemList} />
    <div style={{ marginTop: "24px" }}>
      <ListLayout itemList={listLayoutItems} />
    </div>
  </>
);
```

**Reference**: This hybrid approach provides better organization, performance, and responsive layout control compared to nested ListLayout components.

## 5. Configuration and Integration

### 5.1 LSI Configuration
> After implementing the content component, you MUST update the LSI labels for the detail component.
> 
> **LSI Structure Requirements**:
> 1. **Use component uu5Tag as keys**: The LSI structure must use the component's uu5Tag as the main key
> 2. **Follow hierarchical structure**: Organize labels by component hierarchy
> 3. **Use descriptive keys**: Make keys descriptive of their content
> 4. **Include all display fields**: Add labels for every field displayed in the detail component
> 
> **Correct LSI Structure Example**:
> ```json
> {
>   "UuApp.Module.Detail.View": {
>     "title": "Module Details",
>     "subtitle": "View module information",
>     "info": "Detailed information about the selected module",
>     "loading": "Loading module details...",
>     "error": "Error loading module details"
>   },
>   "UuApp.Module.Detail.Content": {
>     "field1": "Field 1 Label",
>     "field2": "Field 2 Label",
>     "nestedObject": "Nested Object Label",
>     "nestedField1": "Nested Field 1 Label",
>     "nestedField2": "Nested Field 2 Label"
  }
}
```

**Purpose**: Ensures LSI labels are properly structured and accessible using component uu5Tags for internationalization support.

